EXT. CANYON RIM WAGON CAMP - TWILIGHT

A dry arroyo cuts the earth below, red walls blackening in the dusk. A single canvas-topped wagon, wheels sunk to the hubs. A cookfire sputters, spitting coals. Beyond the light, ridge after ridge disappears into purple.

SHERIFF EZEKIEL TOLLIVER, 50s, long coat dusted white, sits on an overturned crate with a tin plate of beans, eating without hunger. Across the fire: MADAME CELESTINE, 40s, in a black traveling dress with a cameo at her throat, mending a glove with quick, exact stitches. ROYCE BARLOW, 30s, wiry, hat pulled low, cleans a rifle he doesn't need to clean. PEARL, 19, red braids, kneels at the wagon tongue feeding a mule from her palm. SILAS, 70s, gray beard, sits against a wheel with his eyes closed, a harmonica loose in his hand.

TOLLIVER
Madame. How far to the river?

CELESTINE
Two days. Maybe three.

TOLLIVER
With the mule or without?

CELESTINE
(not looking up)
Without, we'd be there tomorrow.

ROYCE
The mule's fine.

TOLLIVER
Royce, I didn't ask the mule.

ROYCE
Then I'll say it for the mule. He's fine.

A thin laugh from the wagon wheel. Silas opens one eye.

SILAS
Mule's older than you, Royce. Treat him respectful.

PEARL
(to the mule, murmuring)
Don't listen to them. You're beautiful.

Tolliver sets down his plate. Studies the ridge line. A long silence. Wind lifts embers off the fire and carries them over the rim.

TOLLIVER
(to the group)
Everyone sleep with a boot on.

ROYCE
Why?

TOLLIVER
Because I said.

CELESTINE
(low)
He's seen something.

TOLLIVER
I have not.

CELESTINE
You've felt it, then.

TOLLIVER
(muttering)
Damn this country.

PEARL
Sheriff?

TOLLIVER
Yes, Pearl.

PEARL
There's a light on the north ridge.

They all turn. High on the far ridge, tiny and steady, a single flame flickers.

SILAS
Not a campfire.

ROYCE
Too still.

CELESTINE
It's a lantern. Someone is signaling.

TOLLIVER
To whom?

CELESTINE
To us.

Royce racks the rifle. Pearl stands, one hand still on the mule. Silas pushes himself up, grunting, the harmonica clenched in his fist.

TOLLIVER
Douse the fire.

Pearl kicks dirt over the coals. The dark closes in. Far above, the light on the ridge blinks once, twice, three times.

TOLLIVER (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Nobody answers.

On the ridge, the light goes out.

The canyon is silent. A coyote begins to cry, somewhere very near, and stops mid-note.
